.TH 9SHARE-SETUP 1 "2026" "9share" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
9share-setup \- write the simple, usual configuration of 9share
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B 9share setup
.br
.B 9share setup \-\-export
.I DIR HOST
.RI [ NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-pair ]
.br
.B 9share setup \-\-mount
.I USER NAME DIR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B 9share setup
sets up the usual case of
.BR 9share (8)
in a few questions: share one directory of this machine with another machine, or show on this machine a directory that another one
shares.  It changes nothing until the last question is answered, and does it with the commands that can be typed by hand
.RB ( "9share export" ,
.BR "9share pair" ,
.BR "9share mount" ),
so what it writes is what they write.  Run it as yourself; it asks sudo for the one step that needs root.
.PP
Without arguments, at a terminal, it asks which machine this is (the one that has the files is the first answer and the default).
.TP
.B The machine that has the files
Which directory (default: the current one); which machine should see it, as ssh knows it (a name from
.IR ~/.ssh/config ,
or user@host); what the share is called (default: the directory's name); and whether to make a shared secret as well, which needs
9share installed on that machine already.  Then it adds the export, prints the command to run on the other machine, and makes the
secret if you said yes.  If the secret cannot be made the share is still set up, and the message says what to run later
.RB ( "9share pair" ).
.TP
.B The machine that shows the files
Which account on this machine the other machine logs in as (default: yours); what the share is called there; and where it should
appear (default:
.IR /mnt/NAME ).
Then it adds the mount with
.BR "sudo 9share mount" ,
and says what to run on the machine that has the files.
.PP
With arguments it asks nothing, so that it can be used in a script.
.TP
.BI "\-\-export " "DIR HOST " [ NAME ]
Share DIR with HOST as NAME (default: the directory's name) and make the secret.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pair
With
.BR \-\-export :
do not make a secret.  The share trusts ssh alone.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mount " "USER NAME DIR"
Show USER's share NAME at DIR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when what was asked was done (a secret that could not be made is not a failure); 1 when it could not be; 2 for a wrong command line,
and when there are no arguments and no terminal to ask.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/9share/9share.conf
.TQ
.I /etc/9share/9share.conf
Where the export and the mount are written.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR 9share (8),
.BR 9share.conf (5)
